{
 "id": "2019-kim-fear",
 "name": "Fear of Campus Nightscapes: Built vs Nature-Dominant, With and Without a Person",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Kim, Cheon & Kang (2019), Sustainability 11:233, \"Use of Electroencephalography (EEG) for the Analysis of Emotional Perception and Fear to Nightscapes\". Each participant is randomly assigned to one image group, as in the paper. The built nightscape group (BNIG) rates three built scenes (buildings, roads, walls); the nature-dominant group (NNIG) rates three nature-dominant scenes (fields, forest, parkland). Each scene appears once without and once with an adult figure, giving six photographs per participant. For each photograph participants rate perceived fear from 1 (very safe) to 7 (very fearful). The 12 photographs are the authors' own night photographs of the Virginia Tech campus, taken from Appendix A (CC BY 4.0). They are used with credit and the template is taken down if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) EEG (Emotiv EPOC) is not recorded; only the fear rating is kept. (2) The paper does not report viewing time or image order; the template is self-paced and shows the six images in random order. (3) The appendix prints the photographs at about 400 × 300 px. (4) Background questions (gender, age group, student/faculty/staff) follow the sample description; the paper recruited 60 Virginia Tech students, professors and staff.",
